{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the variance of each lottery and prefers the one with higher variance. Variance can be calculated from the outcomes and their probabilities. For Lottery A: outcome is 5 Euros with 100% probability, so variance = (5 - 5)² × 1 = 0. For Lottery B: outcomes are 5000 Euros (0.1%) and 0 Euros (99.9%), so the expected value is 5000 × 0.001 + 0 × 0.999 = 5 Euros. Variance = (5000 - 5)² × 0.001 + (0 - 5)² × 0.999 = (4995)² × 0.001 + 25 × 0.999 ≈ 24,950,025 × 0.001 + 24.975 ≈ 24,950.025 + 24.975 = 24,975. Thus, Lottery B has a much higher variance than Lottery A (24,975 vs. 0). The decision reason prefers the lottery with higher variance, which would be Lottery B. This reason is applicable to the decision problem as variance can be computed from the given outcomes and probabilities.",
